# sha256.poke -- read stdin, write the SHA-256 digest to stdout.
#
#   cat something | brainstem -- bfi sha256.bf | hx
#
# A file containing nothing but the eight brainfuck instructions reads the
# bytes the shell handed the broker, spawns an interpreter on
# sha256/sha256.bf, feeds them to it, and writes the thirty two byte digest
# back out. It is the ordinary Unix shape -- bytes in, digest out -- and it is
# done entirely by a brainfuck program driving another brainfuck program.
#
# WHY THERE IS A TEMPORARY FILE, since it is the one surprising thing here.
# sha256.bf wants a two byte little-endian LENGTH before the message, and the
# length of a stream is not known until the stream ends. The alternative is to
# buffer every byte on the tape and count them in sixteen bits, which is real
# arithmetic and a lot of pointer discipline. Writing them to a file instead
# lets the FILESYSTEM do the counting: stat reports the size, and the low two
# bytes of that u64 are already exactly the prefix format, little-endian, so
# they are copied byte for byte with no arithmetic at all.
#
# The file is unlinked before the program exits.
#
# A FILE LARGER THAN 65535 BYTES IS REFUSED rather than hashed wrongly. Only
# the low two bytes of the size can reach sha256.bf, so a larger file would
# hash its length modulo 65536 and print a plausible, wrong digest. The six
# high bytes are checked and the program exits 1 if any is set.
#
# COST, BECAUSE IT IS THE REAL LIMIT. sha256.bf is about 1.2 billion
# interpreter instructions per 64 byte block -- roughly two seconds. A
# kilobyte is half a minute. A megabyte is about nine hours. Hash small
# things.
#
# TAPE MAP
# cell 0 is the working cell: every EMIT and every READ uses it.
# cell 1 holds the status byte of a read, which drives the relay loops.
# cell 2 is the relay loop's continue flag.
# cell 3 holds one byte in flight between a read and a write.
# cell 4 is the relay loop's branch flag.
# cells 5 and 6 hold the low two bytes of the file size.
# cell 7 counts the non-zero high bytes of the size: zero means it fits.
# cell 8 is scratch for those high bytes.
# cell 9 is the digest byte counter, thirty two down to zero.
#
# INDENTATION IS LOAD BEARING. A comment indented by two or more spaces
# annotates the frame that follows it; a flush one, like this, is the file
# talking about itself. brainstem's tools/bsframe reads a skeleton by that
# rule.
#
# HANDLES ARE ALLOCATED BEFORE ANYTHING IS CLOSED, deliberately. brainstem
# reuses the lowest free slot and bumps its generation, so closing early would
# make every later handle carry a generation this file would have to track.
# Nothing here is closed until every handle exists.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from brainstem's ABI.md sections 3, 7.10 to 7.23.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10, magic "BSTM", want major 1, want minor 0, flags 0
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 107
#   pipe  op 0e, flags 0.  Handles 4 (read) and 5 (write): this program writes
#   the message into 5 and the child reads it from 4.
EMIT 0e 02 00 00 00
READ 11
#   pipe again.  Handles 6 (read) and 7 (write): the child writes the digest
#   into 7 and this program reads it from 6.
EMIT 0e 02 00 00 00
READ 11
#   open  op 11, dir ffffffff, flags WRITE|CREATE|TRUNC = 0x0016, mode 0644 =
#   0x01a4, path "bfsha.tmp".  Handle 8, the temporary file for writing.
EMIT 11 11 00 ff ff ff ff 16 00 a4 01 62 66 73 68 61 2e 74 6d 70
READ 7
#   open  op 11 again, flags READ = 0x0001, mode 0, same path.  Handle 9, the
#   same file for reading. Opened NOW, before anything is closed, so that the
#   handle numbers stay simple.
EMIT 11 11 00 ff ff ff ff 01 00 00 00 62 66 73 68 61 2e 74 6d 70
READ 7
#   spawn  op 0f, len 0x29 = 41.
#     dir ffffffff meaning the broker's working directory, flags 0,
#     nfdmap 2, nargv 2, nenv 0, reserved 0
EMIT 0f 29 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 02 02 00 00
#     the descriptor map: child fd 0 gets handle 4, child fd 1 gets handle 7.
#     ANY CHILD DESCRIPTOR NOT NAMED HERE IS CLOSED, so the child gets these
#     two and nothing else.
EMIT 00 04 00 00 00
EMIT 01 07 00 00 00
#     the path, then argv: "bfi", "bfi", "sha256.bf". No environment at all:
#     sha256.bf reads an exact, known byte count and never reads past it, so
#     BFI_EOF cannot matter to it.
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 09 00 73 68 61 32 35 36 2e 62 66
#     the reply is a process handle: 10
READ 7
#   close the outer copies of the child's two ends.  Without this the child
#   never sees end of input and this program never sees end of file.
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 07 00 00 00
READ 3
#   THE FIRST RELAY: the broker's stdin, handle 1, into the temporary file,
#   handle 8, one byte at a time until end of file.
#
#   The loop is driven by the STATUS byte of each read, not by a count. A read
#   of one byte returns OK and four bytes when there is a byte, and END with a
#   zero length -- three bytes -- when there is not. Both shapes begin with a
#   status and a two byte length, so those three are always taken; only the
#   data byte is conditional, which is what the branch flag is for.
>>+<<
>>
LOOP
<<
#   read  op 0a, handle 1, one byte, flags 0
EMIT 0a 08 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
>,<,,
>>>>+<<<
[>>>[-]<[-]>>[-]<<<[-]]
>>>
LOOP
<,<<<
#   write  op 0b, handle 8, flags 0, the byte just read
EMIT 0b 07 00 08 00 00 00 00 00
>>>.<<<
READ 5
>>>>[-]
END
<<
END
<<
#   stat  op 13, dir ffffffff, flags 0, path "bfsha.tmp".  Thirty five bytes
#   back: status, two length bytes, then the thirty two byte record.
EMIT 13 0f 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 62 66 73 68 61 2e 74 6d 70
READ 3
#   the record: type, readable, writable, executable
READ 4
#   THE LOW TWO BYTES OF THE SIZE, saved in cells 5 and 6. This is the whole
#   trick: size is a little-endian u64 and sha256.bf wants a little-endian
#   u16, so the first two bytes of one are the whole of the other. No
#   arithmetic, just two commas.
>>>>>,>,<<<<<<
#   the six high bytes of the size, each ORed into cell 7. Any of them set
#   means the file does not fit in a u16 and hashing it would print a
#   plausible wrong answer.
>>>>>>>>,[<+>[-]],[<+>[-]],[<+>[-]],[<+>[-]],[<+>[-]],[<+>[-]]<<<<<<<<
#   the rest of the record: mtime, mode and the reserved bytes
READ 20
#   if cell 7 is set, the file is too large: exit 1 and stop. The broker
#   answers the exit frame and then ends the conversation, which kills the
#   interpreter, so nothing below runs.
>>>>>>>
LOOP
<<<<<<<
EMIT 02 01 00 01
READ 3
>>>>>>>[-]
END
<<<<<<<
#   write  op 0b, handle 5, flags 0, the two size bytes -- sha256.bf's length
#   prefix, straight out of the stat record.
EMIT 0b 08 00 05 00 00 00 00 00
>>>>>.>.<<<<<<
READ 5
#   THE SECOND RELAY, the same loop again: the temporary file, handle 9, into
#   the child's stdin, handle 5.
>>+<<
>>
LOOP
<<
#   read  op 0a, handle 9, one byte, flags 0
EMIT 0a 08 00 09 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
>,<,,
>>>>+<<<
[>>>[-]<[-]>>[-]<<<[-]]
>>>
LOOP
<,<<<
#   write  op 0b, handle 5, flags 0, the byte just read
EMIT 0b 07 00 05 00 00 00 00 00
>>>.<<<
READ 5
>>>>[-]
END
<<
END
<<
#   close handle 5, so the child sees end of input
EMIT 0c 04 00 05 00 00 00
READ 3
#   THE DIGEST, thirty two bytes, one at a time, from handle 6 to handle 2.
#
#   A read of ONE byte is the only read from a stream that is deterministic:
#   it blocks until there is a byte and then returns exactly it. The first of
#   these is where the child's work is waited on, because it writes nothing
#   until it has hashed.
>>>>>>>>>++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
LOOP
<<<<<<<<<
#   read  op 0a, handle 6, one byte, flags 0
EMIT 0a 08 00 06 00 00 00 01 00 00 00
READ 3
>>>,<<<
#   write  op 0b, handle 2 -- the broker's stdout -- flags 0, one byte
EMIT 0b 07 00 02 00 00 00 00 00
>>>.<<<
READ 5
>>>>>>>>>-
END
<<<<<<<<<
#   wait  op 10, handle 10, flags 0 -- blocking.  state 1 exited, code 0.
EMIT 10 06 00 0a 00 00 00 00 00
READ 7
#   unlink  op 15, dir ffffffff, flags 0, path "bfsha.tmp"
EMIT 15 0f 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 62 66 73 68 61 2e 74 6d 70
READ 3
#   close everything still open: the digest pipe, both file handles, and the
#   process handle. Closing a process handle stops tracking the child; it does
#   not kill it.
EMIT 0c 04 00 06 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 08 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 09 00 00 00
READ 3
EMIT 0c 04 00 0a 00 00 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
